      "      --report <PATH>    Write the run's JSON report to a file",
      "  -h, --help             Print help",
    ],
  },
  "build": {
    "exitCode": 0,
    "stderr": [],
    "stdout": [
      "Build an SDK Level Editor scene into a level directory, as the editor's Build writes it. From the scene's parts it writes level.game, level.env_mod and level.fog_vol, and level.spawn, level_rs.spawn, level.ps_static, level.snd_static, level.snd_env and level.wallmarks where the scene holds them. Given --game-materials it also writes what level compile reads, from the scene's objects: build.prj with the scene's build options, level.ltx, level.hom and level.som where objects occlude, build.details and its atlas build_details.dds where the scene holds details, build.aimap where it holds an AI map, the impostor atlas level_lods.dds and level_lods_nm.dds where a multi-use object has an impostor, and each breakable part as meshes/brkbl#<n>.ogf",
      "",
      "Usage: xrf-cli level build [OPTIONS] --dest <dest> <path>",
      "",
      "Arguments:",
      "  <path>  Path to the scene's .level; its parts are read from the directory beside it named after it",
      "",
      "Options:",
      "  -d, --dest <dest>",
      "          Path of the level directory to write the files into",
      "  -s, --silent",
      "          Turn off logging",
      "      --objects <objects>",
      "          Objects root ($objects$) the scene's objects are read from, repeatable, each object from the first root holding it; by default the objects directory beside the scene's levels directory, where level decompile writes them",
      "  -v, --verbose",
      "          Turn on verbose logging",
      "      --json",
      "          Write the run's JSON report to stdout, moving human output to stderr",
      "      --textures <textures>",
      "          Textures root ($game_textures$) the detail objects' textures are merged from into build_details.dds, and the multi-use objects' impostor strips (lod/lod_<object>.dds and _nm) into level_lods.dds and level_lods_nm.dds; by default gamedata/textures beside the scene's rawdata directory, where level decompile writes them",
      "      --game-materials <game-materials>",
      "          Game material library, gamemtl.xr, the objects' surfaces name their game materials from; with it, the build writes build.prj, level.ltx and the occluders",
      "      --report <PATH>",
      "          Write the run's JSON report to a file",
      "      --sdk-impostor-atlas",
      "          Keep the impostor atlas within SDK 0.7's 2048 by 2048, refusing a level of more than 128 impostors as that Level Editor does; by default it grows to 4096 a side, as Anomaly's largest levels ship it",
      "  -f, --force",
      "          Overwrite the files the build writes where the directory already holds them, and remove those of its own an earlier build wrote that this one does not; without it, a directory holding one is refused before the scene is read",
      "  -h, --help",
      "          Print help",
    ],
  },
  "compile": {
    "exitCode": 0,
    "stderr": [],
    "stdout": [
      "Compile a level directory that level build wrote into the files the game loads. The steps run in this process, in order: geometry (level, level.geom, level.geomx and level.cform, as xrLC builds them, and the build.cform and build.lights the later steps read), light (the light of the vertices, lightmaps and terrain, baked in the same pass as the geometry, so it runs only with geometry; without it the level gets flat neutral light), details (level.details lit from build.details, as xrDO_Light lights it) and ai (level.ai from build.aimap, as xrAI -f compiles it, then checked as a graph). Before any step runs, the compile checks that every input of the chosen steps is there, and a .thm for every texture build.prj names",
      "",
      "Usage: xrf-cli level compile [OPTIONS] <path>",
      "",
      "Arguments:",
      "  <path>  Path to the level directory, <levels>/<level>, that level build wrote into",
      "",
      "Options:",
      "  -s, --silent                 Turn off logging",
      "      --steps <steps>          Steps to run, comma-separated; they always run in the order geometry, light, details, ai. Every step by default; light runs only with geometry [possible values: geometry, light, details, ai]",
      "      --gamedata <gamedata>    Game data directory to read gamemtl.xr, shaders_xrlc.xr and the textures from; by default the gamedata directory holding the level's levels directory",
      "  -v, --verbose                Turn on verbose logging",
      "      --json                   Write the run's JSON report to stdout, moving human output to stderr",
      "      --textures <textures>    Textures root searched for the .thm of every texture build.prj names, and the image of each texture with alpha, before the game data's textures directory, which is always read last; repeatable, each file taken from the first root holding it",
      "  -f, --force                  Overwrite what the steps write where the level directory already holds it (the geometry's files and lightmap pages, level.details, level.ai), and remove the lightmap pages of an earlier compile past this one's; without it, a directory holding one is refused before any step runs. A replaced level.ai keeps its identity while its graph is unchanged, so the level's spawn still loads",
      "      --report <PATH>          Write the run's JSON report to a file",
      "      --backend <backend>      What traces the rays of the light, details and ai steps: auto uses the GPU where it supports ray tracing, else the CPU; gpu uses the GPU only, on D3D12 where dxc/dxcompiler.dll is beside the CLI, else on Vulkan; cpu uses the CPU, the reference result [default: auto] [possible values: cpu, gpu, auto]",
      "      --ai-covers <ai-covers>  Covers the ai step computes: full traces them against build.cform, as xrAI -f does; none sets every cover to 0, as xrAI -draft does, and needs no build.cform, for a map to walk before the geometry is compiled [default: full] [possible values: none, full]",
      "      --quality <quality>      How finely the light step lights the level: scene uses build.prj's own sky, sun, lightmap density and samples; draft is a quick estimate, regenerating the sky and sun (also those written to build.lights) at quality 1, lighting lightmaps at 3 texels a metre with one sample each, and each multi-use object, such as a tree, from one sample [default: scene] [possible values: scene, draft]",
      "  -j, --jobs <JOBS>            How much of the machine to use: 'auto', a worker count, or a share such as '50%' [default: auto]",
      "  -h, --help                   Print help",
    ],
  },
  "decompile": {
    "exitCode": 0,
    "stderr": [],
    "stdout": [
      "Decompile a compiled level's directory into an SDK Level Editor scene (.level and its parts), with a .level.xrf.json provenance sidecar beside it. The scene holds the parts the build passes through (particles, sounds, sound environments, fog volumes, wall marks, patrol paths, respawn points, environment modifiers and entity spawns), the level's glows and lights, and its static geometry as one .object per sector with the sectors and portals",
      "",
      "Usage: xrf-cli level decompile [OPTIONS] --dest <dest> <path>",
      "",
      "Arguments:",
      "  <path>  Path to the level's directory, holding level, build.lights, level.game, level.ps_static and the rest",
      "",
      "Options:",
      "  -d, --dest <dest>",
      "          Path of the .level to write; its parts go in the directory beside it named after it, its sidecar beside it, and its name is the level's",
      "  -s, --silent",
      "          Turn off logging",
      "      --objects <objects>",
      "          Objects root ($objects$) the scene's .object files are written under, at levels/<level>/; by default the objects directory beside the destination's directory, as the SDK lays out rawdata/levels and rawdata/objects",
      "  -v, --verbose",
      "          Turn on verbose logging",
      "      --json",
      "          Write the run's JSON report to stdout, moving human output to stderr",
      "      --textures <textures>",
      "          Game textures root ($game_textures$) the textures the objects need for the build, such as the tree models' impostor strips under lod/, are written under; by default gamedata/textures beside the destination's rawdata directory, as the SDK lays out editors/rawdata and editors/gamedata",
      "      --report <PATH>",
      "          Write the run's JSON report to a file",
      "      --surfaces <surfaces>",
      "          How the geometry's surfaces are dressed: infer each game material from the collision face beneath and a compiler shader fitting what the level shows, or give every surface the editor's defaults [default: infer] [possible values: infer, defaults]",
      "      --game-materials <game-materials>",
      "          Game material library naming the collision materials; gamemtl.xr in the level's gamedata by default",
      "      --compiler-shaders <compiler-shaders>",
